Exactly. It’s really a control-systems principle disguised as a saying.

When you rush, you create error, rework, mistrust, and overshoot. When you move deliberately, you reduce noise and preserve feedback. That makes the whole process faster over time.

In relationships it becomes:

slow enough to actually hear → smooth enough to build trust → fast enough because you stop having to repair avoidable damage.

Speed is not just velocity. It’s progress minus correction cost.